call_details.response:
The board state has neither player able to win on the immediate turn, and neither possesses a ready open three. Exhaustive enumeration of all 4-in-a-row windows shows that the only unblocked winning lines that can be completed in a modest number of moves are:

- X’s diagonal (2,3)–(3,4)–(4,5)–(5,6) and the two horizontal threats on rows 3 and 1 that share squares with it;
- O’s horizontal on row 4 that collides with the same squares, O’s diagonal (0,3)–(1,4)–(2,5)–(3,6), and O’s several overlapping horizontals on row 0.

The square (1,4) lies on three of those lines simultaneously. Occupying it with X therefore:

- converts X’s row-1 window into a clean 2-x / 2-empty configuration (the seed of a future open three);
- permanently blocks O’s only free diagonal;
- leaves the newly unlocked square (0,4) under X’s control, because X can still answer any subsequent attempt by O to build a triple on row 0.

All other legal columns either surrender the same square to O, open a poisoned parity battle on columns 5-6 that O can turn into a win, or make no progress on any unblocked line. Consequently the single move that simultaneously improves X’s own threats, eliminates O’s most dangerous counter-threat, and preserves initiative is column 4.

Final Answer: 4

